        "original_id": "9e9f45c4-0374-549b-be71-cada7c9d986a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Joel Chandler Harris, James Baskett\n- Characters: Uncle Remus, Br'er Rabbit, Br'er Fox, Tar-Baby\n- Works: Song of the South, Uncle Remus stories, \"Zip-a-Dee-Doo-Dah\"\n- Companies: The Walt Disney Company\n\nFacts:\n- The Tar-Baby was the second Uncle Remus story published in 1880.\n- The Tar-Baby story is about a doll made of tar and turpentine used by Br'er Fox to entrap Br'er Rabbit.\n- Song of the South is based on the collection of Uncle Remus stories as adapted by Joel Chandler Harris.\n- In 1946, The Walt Disney Company adapted Br'er Rabbit for the animated motion picture Song of the South.\n- In 1946, James Baskett portrayed Uncle Remus and sang \"Zip-a-Dee-Doo-Dah\" in the Disney feature film Song of the South.\n- James Baskett was an American actor born on February 16, 1904, and died on July 9, 1948.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
